abstract = { Rust is a modern programming language designed for strict memory safety and concurrency safety. The foreign function interface (FFI) in Rust enables interactions with other languages like C and C++, which is crucial in systems development. However, Rust FFI bypasses the language’s safety guarantees and can further lead to memory safety issues. Prior studies have examined temporal memory safety issues in Rust foreign function interactions, but spatial memory safety bugs—especially out-of-bounds memory access vulnerabilities—remain unexplored, especially in the real world. We find that such bugs arise when Rust code incorrectly passes data or metadata across FFI boundaries, and C/C++ code subsequently performs memory accesses beyond the allocated memory bounds of the passed data. These bugs can result in severe security consequences, such as crashes, sensitive information leakage, and even arbitrary code execution. In this paper, we propose Rover, the first hybrid analysis approach to systematically detecting out-of-bounds memory access vulnerabilities in Rust foreign function interactions. We design novel cross-language static data-flow analysis to effectively and efficiently identify and track the operations of memory allocation, data passing, and memory accesses. With this analysis, Rover first applies a static bug detector to identify out-of-bounds memory access bug candidates and validation contexts like bug-triggering call chains. It then incorporates dynamic validation that automatically constructs test harnesses to confirm true bugs in real executions, thereby demonstrating security consequences. Our evaluation shows that Rover detects 137 new out-of-bounds memory access findings in the top 6,000 Rust FFI packages, including 118 vulnerabilities reachable from safe APIs and 19 unsafe-API soundness issues, with ten RustSec IDs assigned. },
keywords = {software security;program analysis;vulnerability detection;vulnerability validation;rust security},
